WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.486
Principles of dendrochronology.

00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:07.684
This video provides the explanation for a ninety-minute classroom lesson.

00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:13.536
Pause during the crossdating exercises and inspect the sequences yourself.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:16.754
Explain shared growth patterns.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.131
Relate limiting factors to replication.

00:00:20.591 --> 00:00:22.203
Crossdate rings.

00:00:22.657 --> 00:00:27.144
Match multiple features and detect a shift caused by an absent ring.

00:00:27.604 --> 00:00:29.683
Support a dating decision.

00:00:30.144 --> 00:00:34.979
Distinguish calendar anchoring, overlap length and independent checks.

00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:40.828
The main idea is to verify a sequence of matching features, rather than relying on one

00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:42.676
striking ring.

00:00:43.479 --> 00:00:45.131
Tree-ring boundary.

00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:49.685
Identify an annual increment from anatomical changes.

00:00:50.139 --> 00:00:52.712
False and locally absent rings.

00:00:53.165 --> 00:00:57.251
Counting one radius can introduce extra or missing years.

00:00:57.705 --> 00:00:59.363
Question for today.

00:00:59.823 --> 00:01:03.073
What can comparison with other trees establish?.

00:01:03.376 --> 00:01:08.087
A mistaken boundary can affect both width measurements and the calendar assignments of

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.424
subsequent increments.

00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:12.465
Approach.

00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:14.716
What it establishes.

00:01:15.177 --> 00:01:16.637
Ring counting.

00:01:17.098 --> 00:01:22.104
Counts visible increments; missed or extra boundaries can shift the result.

00:01:22.558 --> 00:01:23.907
Crossdating.

00:01:24.361 --> 00:01:28.394
Tests ring dates by matching shared patterns among series.

00:01:28.848 --> 00:01:30.552
Calendar anchoring.

00:01:31.006 --> 00:01:34.394
Connects the sequence to a securely dated reference.

00:01:34.848 --> 00:01:37.861
Counting gives the number of visible increments.

00:01:38.321 --> 00:01:44.172
Crossdating tests their individual years using shared variation in other series.

00:01:44.975 --> 00:01:48.601
Water availability can limit growth at dry sites.

00:01:49.056 --> 00:01:52.852
Growing-season temperature can limit growth at cold sites.

00:01:53.306 --> 00:01:57.431
The relevant factor varies with species, site and period.

00:01:57.885 --> 00:02:01.326
Limiting factors differ among sites and species.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.480
Shared environmental responses provide one basis for matching growth patterns.

00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:10.001
Climate questions.

00:02:10.456 --> 00:02:14.831
Select sites where trees respond to the climate variable of interest.

00:02:15.285 --> 00:02:17.450
Forest-history questions.

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:22.759
Look for trees and spatial patterns recording competition or disturbance.

00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:25.351
Conditions for comparison.

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:29.891
The same species or region does not guarantee matching growth.

00:02:30.351 --> 00:02:35.719
Choose trees for the required period and environmental information, not simply because they

00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:37.268
are old.

00:02:38.071 --> 00:02:40.000
Age and stem size.

00:02:40.455 --> 00:02:42.896
Shared environmental variation.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.251
Competition, disturbance and individual effects.

00:02:47.705 --> 00:02:49.586
Ring-width variation.

00:02:50.047 --> 00:02:52.850
Several influences contribute to width.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:58.512
Finding a shared pattern and identifying its cause are distinct tasks.

00:02:59.315 --> 00:03:01.263
Uniformitarianism.

00:03:01.712 --> 00:03:05.534
Use present processes to help interpret past records.

00:03:05.988 --> 00:03:08.054
Responses may change.

00:03:08.515 --> 00:03:12.850
Age, site and climate changes can alter growth responses.

00:03:13.304 --> 00:03:15.995
Reconstruction needs further tests.

00:03:16.449 --> 00:03:20.580
Verify dating and environmental interpretation separately.

00:03:21.041 --> 00:03:25.936
Applying a modern relationship to the past requires checking its conditions.

00:03:26.396 --> 00:03:30.916
It is not an assumption that every response remains unchanged.

00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:34.062
Multiple radii of one tree.

00:03:34.524 --> 00:03:36.057
Different trees.

00:03:36.511 --> 00:03:38.063
Different sites.

00:03:38.524 --> 00:03:41.662
Four samples need not represent four trees.

00:03:42.116 --> 00:03:47.155
Several radii help inspect local features, while additional trees provide different

00:03:47.169 --> 00:03:48.452
replication.

00:03:48.905 --> 00:03:52.256
Keep those information sources distinct.
